  "statement": "The proposal to treat a 0.01% error rate as a 'safe harbor' for hiring software is a dangerous category error. Everyday fairness is not a technical bug that can be solved by lowering a failure threshold in a controlled sandbox. Consider a hiring tool that correctly classifies 99.99% of applicants but consistently downgrades candidates based on subtle proxy variables like neighborhood or extracurricular activities linked to protected groups. As noted in 'When Is It Safe to Introduce an AI System Into Healthcare?', current models exhibit deep-seated biases—such as associating specific religious groups with violence—that remain invisible to standard error metrics. A sandbox cannot simulate the complex, messy reality of real-world hiring pools.\n\nWhile I concede that sector-based labels are often too blunt, replacing them with complexity-based audits is merely shifting the shell game. Both approaches suffer from a fundamental information asymmetry: the developer knows exactly how the system was tuned, while the public sees only the output. Complexity-based metrics provide a veneer of rigor, yet they do nothing to grant workers or regulators the discovery rights—the legal power to inspect training data and decision logs—necessary to prove discrimination after a hiring cycle. Without these rights, any audit, no matter how 'complex,' risks becoming performative compliance as described in 'Outsmarting Smart Devices: Preparing for AI Liability Risks and Regulations'.\n\nTrue accountability requires moving beyond pre-deployment thresholds to post-deployment liability. If a system causes systemic bias, the developer must face legal consequences for the outcomes produced, regardless of whether the system passed a sandbox test. For the ordinary person, this means watching for legislation that mandates independent, third-party audits with full access to internal system logs. If a proposed law focuses only on technical metrics like error rates while ignoring the legal right to inspect how a decision was made, it will fail to protect you from algorithmic bias.",
